✅ 8. Daemon Thread vs User Thread

8.1 Definition

User Thread
🔴 A User Thread is a normal thread that performs the main work of an application.
🔴 The JVM waits for all user threads to finish before shutting down.
By default, every thread you create is a User Thread.

Examples:
🔴 Main Thread
🔴 Payment Processing Thread
🔴 Database Thread
🔴 File Upload Thread

Daemon Thread

🔴 A Daemon Thread is a background service thread.
🔴 It supports user threads by performing auxiliary tasks.
🔴 The JVM does not wait for daemon threads to finish.
🔴 When all user threads complete, the JVM terminates, and any remaining daemon threads are stopped automatically.

Examples:
🔴 Garbage Collector (GC)
🔴 JIT Compiler helper threads
🔴 Reference Handler
🔴 Finalizer-related background services (older JVMs)


8.2 Why Do We Need Daemon Threads?

Consider a background logging service.

Application
↓
User Requests
↓
Logger Thread

If the application finishes its work, should it wait forever for the logger?
No.
The logger is only supporting the application.

So it should terminate automatically when the application ends.
That's exactly what a daemon thread does.

8.3 JVM Shutdown Rule

The JVM shuts down when:
🔴 There are no User Threads left running.
🔴 It does not care whether daemon threads are still running.

Flow:

User Threads Running
↓
JVM Continues
↓
Last User Thread Finishes
↓
JVM Terminates
↓
All Daemon Threads Stop Automatically

8.4 Methods Used

🔴 Make a Thread Daemon
thread.setDaemon(true);

8.5 Syntax
Thread thread = new Thread(task);
thread.setDaemon(true);
thread.start();


8.6 How Daemon Threads Work Internally

JVM Starts
├── Main Thread (User)
├── Worker Thread (User)
└── GC Thread (Daemon)
↓
Main Thread Ends
↓
Worker Thread Ends
↓
Only GC Thread Remains
↓
JVM Stops
↓
GC Thread Stops Automatically

